    "reply": "The condition is designated as Lynch syndrome [7]. Multiple genetic loci are linked to the disease, including mutL homolog 1 [10]. Specific mismatch repair genes such as mutS homolog 2 are centrally documented [11].\n\n## Associated Genes\n\nThe disorder is linked to mutS homolog 6 [8]. Another associated gene is PMS1 homolog 2, mismatch repair system component [14]. Additional genomic components include epithelial cell adhesion molecule [9]. A related factor is mutL homolog 3 [6].\n\n## Clinical Trials\n\nClinical investigations include Implementation of a New Strategy to Identify HNPCC Patients [1]. Another registered study is Registry for Women Who Are At Risk Or May Have Lynch Syndrome [5]. Psychosocial aspects have also been evaluated in Psychosocial Aspects of Genetic Testing for Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colon Cancer [3].\n\n## Context and Literature\n\nMedical genetics cataloging designates the entity as Lynch syndrome [19]. Biomedical research literature includes publication 21508689 [21]. Another documented study is publication 34798986 [29].",
