    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.01157625,
    "reply": "The investigated condition is designated as Lynch syndrome [7]. Causal and associated loci identified across NCBI include mutL homolog 1 [10], mutS homolog 2 [11], mutS homolog 6 [8], PMS1 homolog 2, mismatch repair system component [14], and epithelial cell adhesion molecule [9]. Multiple clinical trials evaluate screening, consent, registries, and behavioral outcomes for this condition [1][5].\n\n## Genetic Loci\n\nThe primary mismatch repair gene mutL homolog 1 corresponds to NCBIGene:4292 [10]. The related mismatch repair gene mutS homolog 2 is indexed as NCBIGene:4436 [11]. The mutS homolog 6 component is identified by NCBIGene:2956 [8]. The locus PMS1 homolog 2, mismatch repair system component is represented by NCBIGene:5395 [14]. Additional matched loci include epithelial cell adhesion molecule, designated NCBIGene:4072 [9]. The locus mutL homolog 3 is indexed as NCBIGene:27030 [6]. The gene PMS1 homolog 1, mismatch repair system component is recorded as NCBIGene:5378 [13]. Other associated entries are neurofibromin 1, designated NCBIGene:4763 [12], ribosomal protein S20, designated NCBIGene:6224 [15], transforming growth factor beta receptor 2, designated NCBIGene:7048 [16], and cadherin 1, designated NCBIGene:999 [17].\n\n## Clinical Investigations\n\nDiagnostic screening strategies are evaluated in Implementation of a New Strategy to Identify HNPCC Patients [1]. Regional cohorts and applications are studied in Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer in Taiwan-Related Genetic Study and Clinical Applications [2]. Patient counseling considerations are addressed in Psychosocial Aspects of Genetic Testing for Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colon Cancer [3]. Alternative consent methodologies are tested in Educational CD-ROM Compared With Standard Informed Consent for Patients With Colorectal Cancer or a Family History of Colorectal Cancer [4]. Longitudinal tracking of high-risk populations is conducted in Registry for Women Who Are At Risk Or May Have Lynch Syndrome [5].\n\n## Literature and Disease Concepts\n\nThe disease entity is indexed under the medgen title Lynch syndrome [19]. Key PubMed articles include the publication titled Lynch syndrome. [18] with 21508689 [21], as well as Differentiating Lynch-like from Lynch syndrome. [22] with 24468183 [24]. Further literature includes Lynch Syndrome-Associated Colorectal Cancer. [25] with 30134129 [27], Identification of Lynch Syndrome. [28] with 34798986 [29], and Lynch Syndrome Genetics and Clinical Implications. [30]. Related literature entities comprise Colorectal Neoplasms Hereditary Nonpolyposis [23], Lynch syndrome I (site-specific colonic cancer) [20], and Lynch Syndrome II [26].",
